
<p>Apple Newsroom:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>Apple today announced the opening of Apple Music Hall, a brand-new
state-of-the-art live music venue in London’s storied Battersea
Power Station, designed to connect artists and fans through
bespoke, intimate performances unlike anywhere else.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>Apple might be the most interesting architecture company in the world this century. They have a core look and feel they apply to their own facilities built from the ground up (e.g., Apple Park, <a href="https://www.apple.com/retail/michiganavenue/">certain</a> flagship retail stores), but they also have <a href="https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2018/11/apple-champs-elysees-to-open-on-famed-parisian-avenue-this-sunday/">tremendous respect</a> for <a href="https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2019/05/apple-carnegie-library-opens-saturday-in-washington-dc/">historic buildings</a> they take over.</p>

<p>The balconies in Apple Music Hall are bronze, but the lighting (at least in Apple’s photos) is so warm that the balconies look a little like giant Mac Minis.</p>

